From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:38:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 285/342] Revert "libbnetapi: a Wi-Fi scan gives each channel
 some time"

It didn't help. Three cold boots still joined on 2.4 GHz, scans ran at the same
rate as before, and the dwell only keeps the radio off its channel longer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/kits/network/libnetapi/NetworkDevice.cpp | 6 ------
 1 file changed, 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/kits/network/libnetapi/NetworkDevice.cpp b/src/kits/network/libnetapi/NetworkDevice.cpp
index e44c3ac392..b3beebdf04 100644
--- a/src/kits/network/libnetapi/NetworkDevice.cpp
+++ b/src/kits/network/libnetapi/NetworkDevice.cpp
@@ -792,12 +792,6 @@ BNetworkDevice::Scan(bool wait, bool forceRescan)
 		| IEEE80211_IOC_SCAN_ONCE
 		| (forceRescan ? IEEE80211_IOC_SCAN_FLUSH : 0);
 	request.sr_duration = IEEE80211_IOC_SCAN_FOREVER;
-	// In ms. Left at 0 a channel gets no time at all, and access points that
-	// don't answer at once go unseen: those on 5 GHz channels with radar
-	// detection, for one. The supplicant takes a join from this result if it
-	// is recent. 120 ms hears a beacon sent every 102.4 ms.
-	request.sr_mindwell = 20;
-	request.sr_maxdwell = 120;
 	request.sr_nssid = 0;
 
 	status_t status = set_80211(Name(), IEEE80211_IOC_SCAN_REQ, &request,
